A scroll's `direction` reaches the scrolled window through the set
gtk4.sngl declares for it, `_set_Scroll_direction`, whose
`_rtScrollSetDirection` compares the axis as a SNGL string and calls GTK
through the generated wrappers, so the program holds no C string. That sets
the scrolled window's two policies, lays the content out at its natural size
along each axis that scrolls, and reads @reachEnd along x in a scroll moving
sideways.

A scroll given no direction is set "vertical", ui.ScrollDirection's default,
which is how a scroll _rtScrollNew makes already moves.
deny out/go/gtk4/main.go `import "C"` -- the scroll is SNGL calling GTK through the generated wrappers, so the program imports no C
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        gtk4
    }
}

ui.window(title="t") {
    ui.scroll #down {
        ui.text(value="rows")
    }
    ui.scroll #across(direction=horizontal) {
        ui.text(value="columns")
    }
    ui.scroll #either(direction=both) {
        ui.text(value="a page")
    }
}
